Heap allocation across backends
================================

A small corpus that exercises malloc/free paths across every backend
(`interpreter`, `llvm-host`, `svm-host`, `trisc`). Pins audit item #34:
the TRISC runner now ships a built-in bump-allocator shim, so any test
that triggers `new T(...)`, dynamic slice growth, or string interpolation
links and runs end-to-end on `--backend trisc`.

    module sysl.tests.heap_alloc


### `new T(...)` ref-counted box

A heap-allocated struct is the most basic malloc/free trigger. The ref
goes out of scope at the end of the test, decrementing to zero and
calling `free`.

    struct Box
        value: int

    #test
    test_new_box_round_trips() -> unit
        val b = new Box(42)
        assert(b.value == 42, "round-trip")


### `new [n]T` dynamic array

Heap-allocated slices use a separate malloc path (size derived from cap).
The slice's refcount drops to zero at scope end.

    #test
    test_new_dynamic_array() -> unit
        val arr = new [16]int
        var i = 0
        while i < 16
            arr[i] = i * i
            i = i + 1
        assert(arr[0] == 0, "first")
        assert(arr[7] == 49, "middle")
        assert(arr[15] == 225, "last")


### `append` past initial capacity reallocates

`append` grows the underlying buffer by allocating a larger one and
copying. Pushing 64 elements into a 4-cap slice forces several growth
rounds.

    #test
    test_append_grows_slice() -> unit
        val seed = new [4]int
        var s = seed[:0]
        var i = 0
        while i < 64
            s = append(s, i)
            i = i + 1
        assert(len(s) == 64, "len after grows")
        assert(s[0] == 0, "first preserved across grows")
        assert(s[63] == 63, "last value")


### Many small allocations exercise the bump pointer

Allocating 200 small refcount-headed boxes in a loop sanity-checks that
the bump allocator advances correctly and free is a true no-op (no
heap corruption from double-frees, no premature OOM).

    #test
    test_many_small_allocs() -> unit
        var i = 0
        while i < 200
            val b = new Box(i)
            assert(b.value == i, "each box round-trips")
            i = i + 1
